  "worked_solution": "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(G),$ we reduce the matrix $G$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 2 & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}^{\u2032} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, only the $1$st column is a pivot column, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st column from the original matrix is ${g}_{1}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(G)$ is ${{g}_{1}},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
